Here is an embedding of $Q_5$ as rectangle visibility graph in 2 dimensions:
[asy]
unitsize(0.0833333333333333333333333333333333inch);

void drawbox (pair position, pair size, pair scaling, pen p)
{
    pair other = position + size;
    path cube = position--(other.x, position.y)--(other.x, other.y)--(position.x, other.y)--cycle;
    fill(xscale(scaling.x)*yscale(scaling.y)*cube, p);
}

void flatcube (pair scaling, pen p)
{
    drawbox((3, 3), (4, 4), scaling, p);
    drawbox((13, 4), (4, 4), scaling, p);
    drawbox((22, 4), (6, 6), scaling, p);
    drawbox((32, 2), (6, 6), scaling, p);
    drawbox((2, 16), (6, 6), scaling, p);
    drawbox((12, 15), (6, 6), scaling, p);
    drawbox((21, 13), (8, 8), scaling, p);
    drawbox((31, 15), (8, 8), scaling, p);
}

void drawgrid (pair start, pair end, pair spacing, pen p)
{
    for (real x=start.x; x <= end.x; x += spacing.x)
        draw((x, start.y)--(x, end.y), p);
    for (real y=start.y; y <= end.y; y += spacing.y)
        draw((start.x, y)--(end.x, y), p);
}

pen theGreenIAlwaysUse = rgb(0.3,0.7,0.4);
pen theGreyISometimesUse = grey;

drawgrid((-40, -24), (40, 24), (2, 2), theGreyISometimesUse+dotted);

flatcube((1, 1), theGreenIAlwaysUse);
flatcube((-1, 1), theGreenIAlwaysUse);
flatcube((-1, -1), theGreenIAlwaysUse);
flatcube((1, -1), theGreenIAlwaysUse);

[/asy]

By stacking two copies of this graph vertically, we have an embedding of $Q_6$ in 3-space.  Generally, this means that if $d \ge 2$, $m \ge d+3.$

[quote=]Great work, user. That might be the coolest CrowdMath result so far. Can this idea for $Q_{d}$ extend to $k$-visibility graphs?[/quote]

My conjecture is that $m=d$ for $k \ge 1.$  Note that by the above, this is less than the value of $m$ for $k=0.$

This is achievable because you can arrange $2^d$ $d$-dimensional hypercubes into a $d$-dimensonal hypercube formation, as shown here for $d=3.$
[asy]
import three;
currentprojection=perspective(-4/5,1/2,2/3);
surface myCube=shift((1,1,1))*scale3(2)*unitcube;
pen the3DGreenIAlwaysUse = lightgreen;
draw(scale(1,-1,1)*myCube, the3DGreenIAlwaysUse);
draw(scale(-1,1,-1)*myCube, the3DGreenIAlwaysUse);
draw(scale(-1,-1,1)*myCube, the3DGreenIAlwaysUse);
draw(scale(-1,-1,-1)*myCube, the3DGreenIAlwaysUse);
draw(scale(1,1,1)*myCube, the3DGreenIAlwaysUse);
draw(scale(1,1,-1)*myCube, the3DGreenIAlwaysUse);
draw(scale(1,-1,-1)*myCube, the3DGreenIAlwaysUse);
draw(scale(-1,1,1)*myCube, the3DGreenIAlwaysUse);
[/asy]

However $k \ge 1$ visibility graphs are very limiting.  All $d$-dimensional representations of $Q_m$ as a $k \ge 1$ visibility graph have to be representations as a $0$ visibility graph; if two rectangular prisms were blocked by another, and they saw each other because of $k$ visibility, a triangle would be formed between the three rectangles, which is not allowed in a $d$-dimensional hypercube.  

Intuitively, $k$ visibility produces a lot of triangles, which need to be avoided. Since $d$ dimensions allow for 2 "layers" of cubes per axis, no triangles are formed, so I think $d = m.$

