First a little lemma.

[b]Lemma:[/b]
[indent][i]Let $G_{i,j}$ be the $j^{th}$ digit (from the right; indexing from $j=0$) of the [url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gray_code]Gray code[/url] representation of $i$. Then, the $d$-dimensional rectangle $0$-visibility graph formed by cubes of side length $2$ centered at points of the form $$\left(4i,~ G_{i,0},~G_{i,1},~G_{i,2},~\ldots,~G_{i,d-2}\right) \text{ for } 0\le i\le2^d-1$$ is $Q_d.$[/i]

[b]Proof by induction.[/b]

[b]Base case: $d=0$[/b]
[indent]
This case trivially holds, as a single point is a valid representation of $Q_0$ in 0-dimensional space.
[/indent]

[b]Inductive step: $d \Rightarrow d+1$[/b]
[indent]
For any rectangle centered at a point of the form $$\left(4i,~ G_{i,0},~G_{i,1},~G_{i,2}~\ldots,~G_{i,d-2}\right)$$ in $d$-space, we obtain two adjacent rectangles in $d+1$ space with vectors $$\left(8i,~ G_{i,0},~G_{i,1},~G_{i,2}~\ldots,~G_{i,d-2},~i-2\left\lfloor\dfrac i2\right\rfloor\right)$$ and $$\left(8i+4,~ G_{i,0},~G_{i,1},~G_{i,2}~\ldots,~G_{i,d-2},~1+2\left\lfloor\dfrac i2\right\rfloor-i\right),$$ respectively. By induction, all rectangles whose center coordinates end with 0 form a hypercube in $d$-space, likewise all rectangles whose center coordinates end with 1 form another hypercube in $d$-space.  All of these $2^{d+1}$ rectangles now form a representation of $Q_{d+1}$ in $d+1$-space.
[/indent]
[/indent]


