[quote=]Another graph that I wonder about is the $m$-dimensional hypercube $Q_{m}$.[/quote]

Here is a different construction that [url=http://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c382892h1360530p8787987]yet[/url] [url=http://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c382892h1360530p8818577]again[/url] utilizes Gray code, but achieves a better bound of $m \ge d^2+d$ for $d\ge2.$

For any tuple $\left(i_0, i_1, \ldots, i_{d-1}\right)$ with $0 \le i_j \le 2^{d+1}-1 ~ \forall~ j \in \{ 0, 1, \ldots, d-1 \}$ take a rectangle with opposite corners at 
$$\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\begin{bmatrix}
0&0&0&\ldots&0&0&0&{\color{red}1}\\
{\color{red}1}&0&0&\ldots&0&0&0&0\\
0&{\color{red}1}&0&\ldots&0&0&0&0\\
0&0&{\color{red}1}&\ldots&0&0&0&0\\
\vdots&\vdots&\vdots&\ddots&\vdots&\vdots&\vdots&\vdots\\
0&0&0&\ldots&{\color{red}1}&0&0&0\\
0&0&0&\ldots&0&{\color{red}1}&0&0\\
0&0&0&\ldots&0&0&{\color{red}1}&0
\end{bmatrix}^ja_{i_j}~\text{and}~\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\begin{bmatrix}
0&0&0&\ldots&0&0&0&{\color{red}1}\\
{\color{red}1}&0&0&\ldots&0&0&0&0\\
0&{\color{red}1}&0&\ldots&0&0&0&0\\
0&0&{\color{red}1}&\ldots&0&0&0&0\\
\vdots&\vdots&\vdots&\ddots&\vdots&\vdots&\vdots&\vdots\\
0&0&0&\ldots&{\color{red}1}&0&0&0\\
0&0&0&\ldots&0&{\color{red}1}&0&0\\
0&0&0&\ldots&0&0&{\color{red}1}&0
\end{bmatrix}^jb_{i_j},$$

where

$$a_i=\begin{bmatrix}
(d+4)i\\
-G_{i,d-2}G_{i,d-1}+2G_{i,d-2}+G_{i,d-1}\\
G_{i,0}\\
G_{i,1}\\
G_{i,2}\\
\vdots\\
G_{i,d-4}\\
G_{i,d-3}
\end{bmatrix},~ b_i=\begin{bmatrix}
(d+4)i+4\\
G_{i,d-2}-G_{i,d-1}\\
G_{i,0}\\
G_{i,1}\\
G_{i,2}\\
\vdots\\
G_{i,d-4}\\
G_{i,d-3}
\end{bmatrix}.$$

There are $2^{d^2+d}$ such tuples.

Here is this construction applied to $d=2:$
[asy]
pen theGreenIAlwaysUse = rgb(0.3,0.7,0.4);

void boxy (pair x1y1, pair x2y2, pen decoration=theGreenIAlwaysUse) 
{
fill(x1y1--(x2y2.x,x1y1.y)--x2y2--(x1y1.x,x2y2.y)--cycle,decoration);
}

for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i)
    for (int j = 0; j < 8; ++j)
    {
        real i1 = ((int)(i/1)+(int)(i/2))%2;
        real j1 = ((int)(j/1)+(int)(j/2))%2;
        real i2 = ((int)(i/2)+(int)(i/4))%2;
        real j2 = ((int)(j/2)+(int)(j/4))%2;

        pair pointa = 
             (6i+2*j1+j2-j1*j2,
              6j+2*i1+i2-i1*i2);
        pair pointb = 
             (6i+j1-j2+4,
              6j+i1-i2+4);
        boxy(pointa,pointb);
    }
[/asy]

