[quote=]For each $m > 1$, are there any non-bipartite killer-win graphs that are also $C_{2k+1}$-free for each $k \leq m$?[/quote]

Consider the graph with vertices labeled $1$ through $4m+6,$ with edges between a pair of vertices if and only if their labels differ by $1,~ 2m+2,~ 2m+4,~$ or $4m+5.$

For example, the following graph illustrates this graph for $m=8.$
[asy]
size(10cm);
int rotations = 19;
for (int i=0; i<rotations; ++i)
{
    dot(dir(i*360/rotations)); label((string)(i+1), dir(i*360/rotations), -1.5*dir(i*360/rotations));
    dot(2*dir(i*360/rotations)); label((string)(i+rotations+1), 2*dir(i*360/rotations), 1.5*dir(i*360/rotations));
    draw(dir(i*360/rotations) -- dir((i+1)*360/rotations) -- 2*dir(i*360/rotations) -- 2*dir((i+1)*360/rotations) -- cycle);
}
[/asy]

The killer wins by choosing the vertex whose label differs from the cop's by $2m+3$ (i.e., $19$ in the graph above).  Then, wherever the cop moves in his initial move, the killer can get to right away.

[quote=][quote=][quote=]For each $m > 1$, are there any non-bipartite killer-win graphs that are also $C_{2k+1}$-free for each $k \leq m$?[/quote]

Consider the graph with vertices labeled $1$ through $4m+6,$ with edges between a pair of vertices if and only if their labels differ by $1,~ 2m+2,~ 2m+4,~$ or $4m+5.$

For example, the following graph illustrates this graph for $m=8.$
[asy]
size(10cm);
int rotations = 19;
for (int i=0; i<rotations; ++i)
{
    dot(dir(i*360/rotations)); label((string)(i+1), dir(i*360/rotations), -1.5*dir(i*360/rotations));
    dot(2*dir(i*360/rotations)); label((string)(i+rotations+1), 2*dir(i*360/rotations), 1.5*dir(i*360/rotations));
    draw(dir(i*360/rotations) -- dir((i+1)*360/rotations) -- 2*dir(i*360/rotations) -- 2*dir((i+1)*360/rotations) -- cycle);
}
[/asy]

The killer wins by choosing the vertex whose label differs from the cop's by $2m+3$ (i.e., $19$ in the graph above).  Then, wherever the cop moves in his initial move, the killer can get to right away.[/quote]
This construction is excellent. Any idea how to find the maximum number of edges in a non-bipartite killer-win graph with $n$ vertices?[/quote]

[b]Case 1:[/b] $n>5$ is even.
[indent]Consider the complement of the following graph:
[asy]
for (int i=0; i<8; ++i)
{
dot((i,1));
dot((i,0));
draw((i,1)--(i,0));
}
[/asy]
Wherever the cop starts, the killer can choose the vertex that is not adjacent to the cop, and win on his next turn. If there were any more edges than this, the cop would win, as there would be a universal vertex. Therefore, the maximum number of edges in a non-bipartite killer-win graph with $n$ vertices is $$\binom n2-\frac n2+1.$$[/indent]
[indent][b]Case 2:[/b] $n>5$ is odd.
Consider the complement of the following graph:
[asy]
for (int i=0; i<7; ++i)
{
dot((i,1));
dot((i,0));
draw((i,1)--(i,0));
}
draw((6,1)--(7,0.5)--(6,0));
dot((7,0.5));
[/asy]
Wherever the cop starts, the killer can choose a vertex that is not adjacent to the cop, and win on his next turn. If there were any more edges than this, the cop would win, as there would be a universal vertex, or the graph would be the compliment of
[asy]
for (int i=0; i<7; ++i)
{
dot((i,1));
dot((i,0));
draw((i,1)--(i,0));
}
draw((6,1)--(7,0.5));
label("$u$",(6,0),S);
label("$w$",(6,1),N);
label("$v$",(7,0.5),E);
dot((7,0.5));
[/asy]
; the cop starts at $v,$ when the killer chooses $w,$ the cop moves to $u,$ and wins on his next turn. Therefore, the maximum number of edges in a non-bipartite killer-win graph with $n$ vertices is $$\binom n2-\frac {n+1}2.$$[/indent]

[b]Case 3:[/b] $n\le5$
