This looks great, since I think you solved the same question (phrased in a slightly different way) in the thread on extremal functions. 
Is the maximum still $\Theta(n^2)$ for a non-bipartite killer-win graph that is also $C_{2k+1}$-free for each $k \leq m$? Or is it $o(n^2)$?[/quote]

The maximum is still $\Theta(n^2).$ Let $n\ge4m+6.$ Consider the graph with vertices $1$ to $n$ where vertices $u$ and $v$ are connected iff $u-v\equiv\pm1\pmod{2m+3}.$ This graph is killer win; the killer picks a vertex $y$ such that if $x$ is the cop's vertex, $y\equiv x\pmod{2m+3},$ and wins on his next turn.

The number of edges in this graph is $\Theta(n^2),$ so the maximum possible number of edges is also $\Theta(n^2).$

