[quote=]These closure corollaries are great. Do we need to specify that the graphs are connected for the cartesian and strong products, but not necessarily for the tensor product?[/quote]

Although it's a bit harder to see at first glance, we actually don't.

The only disconnected graphs that are cop-win are those where all non-trivial connections occur within the same cop-win component, i.e. a non-trivial cop-win graph plus any number of isolated vertices.  This is cop-win because the cop can play in optimal position in the non-trivial component, and the killer will either play in the same component and lose, or else play in a different component and not have any moves after his initial choice of vertex.
[asy]
size(6cm);
pair A1 = (1,2); pair A2 = (3,-1);
pair B1 = (0,-1); pair B2 = (2,2);
pair C1 = (0,0); pair C2 = (3,0);
path a = A1 -- A2;
path b = B1 -- B2;
path c = C1 -- C2;
pair AB = point(a, intersect(a,b)[0]);
pair BC = point(b, intersect(b,c)[0]);
pair CA = point(c, intersect(c,a)[0]);

draw(a);
draw(b);
draw(c);
dot(A1); dot(A2); dot(AB);
dot(B1); dot(B2); dot(BC);
dot(C1); dot(C2); dot(CA);
dot((-3,2));
dot((-4,1));
dot((-3,0));
dot((-4,-1));
dot((6,2));
dot((7,1));
dot((6,0));
dot((7,-1));
[/asy]
There no others, because if there were two or more disconnected components with at least one edge, then the killer could play in the unoccupied one and never lose.

The Cartesian and strong products of two graphs that do [i]not[/i] fall into the category described here is also not of this category.

